<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" version="2.0"><channel><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link><description>World News Today: Read the latest world news headlines, Current International breaking news world wide. In depth analysis and top news headlines world wide.</description><language>en-gb</language><lastBuildDate>2026-08-14T14:23:26+05:30</lastBuildDate><atom:link type="application/rss+xml" rel="self" href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/rssfeeds/296589292.cms"/><copyright>Copyright:(C) Fri, 14 Aug 2026 14:23:26 IST Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. Ltd, http://in.indiatimes.com/policyterms/1554651.cms</copyright><docs>http://syndication.indiatimes.com/</docs><image><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><url>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/507610.cms</url><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link></image><item><title><![CDATA[Egyptian archaeologists uncover 3 tombs, 5 mummies, and Roman remains]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133231962.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[An Egyptian archaeological mission has uncovered three tombs at the Sheikh Sobi site. These burials include mummies and sarcophagi from the Late and Roman periods. One tomb contained five mummies, dating to the Late Period. The other two tombs are believed to be from the Roman period. These discoveries offer insights into the Bahariya Oasis's ancient communities.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/egyptian-archaeologists-discover-3-tombs-in-bahariya-oasis-with-5-mummies-and-roman-era-remains/articleshow/133231962.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/egyptian-archaeologists-discover-3-tombs-in-bahariya-oasis-with-5-mummies-and-roman-era-remains/articleshow/133231962.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-14T13:20:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133232250,imgsize-224640.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Indian-origin man rejected for H-1B three times, spent $13,000 and formed 700 pages of evidence to stay in US]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133232380.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Sohan Sethi faced multiple H-1B visa rejections after arriving in the United States. He then pursued an O-1 visa, requiring extensive documentation and lawyer fees. Sethi demonstrated extraordinary ability through achievements and industry recognition to secure his stay. This path highlights the challenges faced by skilled professionals seeking to remain in America. He now manages a team, sharing his experience to guide others through the process.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/indian-origin-man-rejected-for-h-1b-visa-three-times-compiled-700-pages-of-evidence-and-spent-13000-to-secure-another-way-to-stay-in-us/articleshow/133232380.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/indian-origin-man-rejected-for-h-1b-visa-three-times-compiled-700-pages-of-evidence-and-spent-13000-to-secure-another-way-to-stay-in-us/articleshow/133232380.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-14T14:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133232553,imgsize-94659.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Great Barrier Reef coral cover rebounds to 35.1% despite bleaching]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133230206.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-2025-coral-cover-in-the-great-barrier-reefs-northern-region-fell-to-30-after-its-largest-annual-decline-on-record-a-year-later-surveys-of-192-reefs-found-it-had-risen-to-35-1-despite-another-bleaching-event/articleshow/133230206.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-2025-coral-cover-in-the-great-barrier-reefs-northern-region-fell-to-30-after-its-largest-annual-decline-on-record-a-year-later-surveys-of-192-reefs-found-it-had-risen-to-35-1-despite-another-bleaching-event/articleshow/133230206.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-14T13:59:46+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133230260,imgsize-525669.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Shakespeare's birth home was nearly shipped to US in 1847; it was saved from a showman's fantasy with a payment of £3,000]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133232772.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In the 1840s, Shakespeare's birthplace in Stratford-upon-Avon faced the threat of sale, as American showman P.T. Barnum aimed to move the historic home to New York. A dedicated committee formed, rallying public donations and support from notable figures like Prince Albert to save the site. Their efforts culminated in the successful acquisition of the house at auction in 1847, ensuring the preservation of this cherished cultural landmark.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/shakespeares-birth-home-was-about-to-be-shipped-to-the-us-in-1847-the-shakespeare-trust-had-to-buy-it-for-3000-to-save-it-from-a-showman-who-wanted-to-make-it-a-theme-park-attraction/articleshow/133232772.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/shakespeares-birth-home-was-about-to-be-shipped-to-the-us-in-1847-the-shakespeare-trust-had-to-buy-it-for-3000-to-save-it-from-a-showman-who-wanted-to-make-it-a-theme-park-attraction/articleshow/133232772.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-14T13:36:05+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133232921,imgsize-119823.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Morocco is testing a 'sponge' pavement that stays 16°F cooler by itself, aims at replacing black asphalt]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133230174.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Morocco is pioneering a novel sponge pavement designed to enhance water management and mitigate heat effects. Unlike conventional black asphalt, this innovative surface enables water to seep through, promoting natural cooling through evaporation when moisture exists. This initiative aims to bolster urban climate resilience and elevate the comfort of residents, with pilot projects underway in Marrakech and Agadir as part of a larger sustainable city initiative.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/morocco-is-testing-a-sponge-pavement-to-replace-black-asphalt-on-roads-in-marrakech-and-agadir-can-run-up-to-16f-cooler-by-itself/articleshow/133230174.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/morocco-is-testing-a-sponge-pavement-to-replace-black-asphalt-on-roads-in-marrakech-and-agadir-can-run-up-to-16f-cooler-by-itself/articleshow/133230174.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-14T13:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclos